Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 12:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] Keep the library's device lists in step with mounts

[AI-generated text: this description was written autonomously by an AI
agent (Claude). Review it before relying on it or submitting it.]

Computer > Removable Devices, and the device lists of the Rekordbox and
Serato features, are built when they are expanded or activated and never
again. A drive plugged in afterwards does not appear, and one pulled out
stays listed, until the node is collapsed and expanded - which on a
touchscreen appliance with no mouse is hard to do and not obvious to
need.

RemovableDeviceWatcher watches the directories devices are mounted under
(/media, /media/$USER and /run/media/$USER on Linux, the same ones those
features already look in). Mounting creates a directory in one of them
and unmounting removes it, so a QFileSystemWatcher on them sees exactly
that, and nothing that happens inside a device. Changes are coalesced
and reported once things have been quiet for half a second. A root that
does not exist yet is picked up when it appears, but never by watching a
top-level directory such as /run. On other platforms there is nothing to
watch and the watcher does nothing.

On a change, BrowseFeature rebuilds Removable Devices the way expanding
it does, and clears the track list if it was showing a folder on a
device that has gone. RekordboxFeature and SeratoFeature look for
devices again without switching the view; a change that arrives while a
search is running is looked for once that search is done.

Their removal loops walked forwards while removing rows, which skipped
the device after each one removed. They walk backwards now, so pulling
two drives at once removes both.

BrowseTableModel::setPath() with an empty path never cleared the list:
the browse thread skips an empty path without clearing the model, so the
previous folder's files stayed listed after selecting Quick Links or
Removable Devices - which the caller's own comment says should clear the
tracks view - and, now, after the device holding that folder had gone.
It clears the model itself.

removabledevicewatcher_test covers mount and unmount being reported, a
burst being reported once, a root that appears later, changes inside a
device being ignored, and an empty root list.

[End of AI-generated text.]

